diary - a private diary in one encrypted file
=============================================

SYNOPSIS

    diary              open the diary: password, then the screen
    diary -n           make a new diary (password twice)
    diary -c CMD       run CMD in the open diary instead of the screen
    diary -f FILE      another diary file than ~/.diary
    diary -s           the password from standard input, for scripts
    diary -h           help

DESCRIPTION

The diary is one file, ~/.diary, encrypted like seal's files (it is
one: unseal opens it too). diary asks for the password, unpacks it
into RAM (a tmpfs only you can read) and shows it. Everything you
write is saved back into ~/.diary after each edit, and when you quit
(q) the copy in RAM is overwritten with zeros and removed. There is no
plain copy anywhere else, on the disk or in RAM.

It holds:

    your days            a page a day, written in Neovim
    your plans           one day, several days, weekly, monthly, yearly
    people and places    a note, more notes (appearance, schedule, ...),
                         their own days, their plans, attached files
    mentions             @mira in any text is about Mira, #bakery about
                         the bakery: it shows on their calendar

Attached files go through clean (../clean) first: their metadata goes
and they get a hash of their own. A file clean doesn't know is
attached only if you say so, and is marked "as-is".

THE SCREEN

The left side is the diary, the right side what is chosen in it, or
the editor (Neovim, else Vim) while you write. Colours are Habamax's,
boxes like btop's.

    1 days      your calendar (green: you wrote that day, teal: plans),
                your days, and what is coming up in the next 60 days
    2 people    everyone; enter opens one: their calendar (their days,
                their plans and every day of yours that mentions
                them), their notes and their files
    3 places    the same for places
    4 search    everything, as you type; enter edits at that line

On a day, the right side shows that day's page, then the plans on it
(yours and everyone's), then what you wrote about others that day.

KEYS

Moving is with the arrows, never letters (the keyboard is not
QWERTY). Moving between the boxes is tmux's: Ctrl-b, then tmux's own
key for its panes and windows, so the Moonlander's tmux layer works.

    Ctrl-b up, down, o    the box above, below, the next one
    Ctrl-b right, left    into the editor, back to the left side
    Ctrl-b n p 1-4        the next, last screen, a screen
    Ctrl-b l              the screen you were on before
    Ctrl-b Ctrl-b         a Ctrl-b for the editor

    1 2 3 4     the screens            home end        first, last
    up down     in a list; on a        pgup pgdn       ten lines; on a
                calendar a week                        calendar a month
    left right  a day back, forward    shift-left      a month back,
                                       shift-right     forward
    t           today                  shift-up        scroll the right
                                       shift-down      side
    enter, e    write or edit          n               new person,
                                                       place or note
    p           a plan on that day     v               mark a day: p
                                                       then plans from
                                                       there to here
    a           attach a file          o               open it
    r           rename it              D               delete (asks)
    esc         back to the list       /               search
    w           save now               !               a shell in the
                                                       diary
    Ctrl-\      to the editor and back, while it is open
    ?           help                   q               save, wipe, quit

In the editor, :wq (or :q) gives the right side back, and diary
saves. While the editor is open, Ctrl-b left (or Ctrl-\) goes to the
left side and Ctrl-b right back, without closing it.

Inside tmux, tmux takes the first Ctrl-b itself: press it twice there
(tmux sends the second one on), as for any program in tmux.

THE FILES IN IT

All plain text, so the shell (!) and grep work on it too:

    days/2026-11-21                 your day
    events                          your plans
    people/mira/note                "key: value" lines, "--", text
    people/mira/notes/appearance    more notes, any name
    people/mira/days/2026-11-28     her day: what you know of it
    people/mira/events              her plans
    people/mira/files/              attachments (their own names)
    people/mira/attached            name, a tab, clean/text/as-is
    places/bakery/...               the same

No endings (.txt): it is all text. A diary from before is renamed
when it opens.

The short name (mira) is the directory's name; @mira finds her. The
note's "name:" line is the full name, and the other "key: value"
lines before "--" show on her box (met, birthday, address, ...).

Plans, one a line in events (# starts a comment):

    2026-11-25 dentist at 10:00                one day
    2026-11-25..2026-11-28 riding trip         several days
    weekly mon,thu chess at 18:00              every week
    monthly 15 rent                            every month
    yearly 03-14 birthday                      every year

A plan that mentions @mira shows on her calendar too.

SETTINGS

~/.system/config/diary/config (linked to diary/config in the dotfiles): the
file, the editor (it falls back to nvim, vim, vi when the one set
isn't there), the programs that open attachments (feh, Sioyek, mpv),
the clean program, the shell. The file explains each.

SAVING, AND WHAT IF IT CRASHES

Each save: a new random salt and key (Argon2id from the password,
which stays in locked memory while the diary is open), the whole
diary written to a hidden file next to ~/.diary, read back and
compared, then the old one becomes ~/.diary.old (one save older,
encrypted too) and the new one ~/.diary. Until then ~/.diary is as it
was.

    diary killed, terminal closed   the copy stays in RAM. The next
                                    diary says so and asks: open it,
                                    save it, or wipe it
    the terminal is closed (HUP)    diary saves and wipes first
    power cut, reboot               RAM is gone; ~/.diary is the last
                                    save (at most the edit in progress
                                    is lost)
    a crash while saving            ~/.diary is untouched
    diary in two terminals          the second one says the diary is
                                    open, and stops

A wrong password, or a damaged file, opens nothing. unseal -f gets out
what can be read of a damaged one (see seal/README).

THE ENCRYPTION

seal's: Argon2id (32 MiB, 2 passes, a new salt each save) and
XChaCha20-Poly1305 in 64 KiB chunks, from Monocypher. The file looks
like random bytes from first to last: no header, no names. Fixed
forever, so unseal can always open it.

WHAT IT CAN'T DO

- While it is open, the diary is a normal directory in RAM: every
  program of yours can read it. Quit when you are done.
- The editor keeps things of its own. With "private yes" (the
  default) diary runs it with -i NONE (no history of what was typed,
  searched or yanked) and puts its undo files, views, backups and swap
  files in RAM next to the diary (XDG_STATE_HOME, and for nvim and vim
  undodir, viewdir, backupdir, directory), wiped when it closes. Undo
  works while the diary is open, but not after you open it again. A
  plugin that writes elsewhere on its own isn't covered.
- Viewers keep things too: Sioyek remembers the files it opened (their
  paths and names), mpv may as well.
- The password is typed where X programs could read it; for the most
  care, open the diary on a text console.
- Forget the password and the diary is gone. Keep a copy of
  ~/.diary somewhere (it is encrypted).
- tmpfs can go to swap when memory runs low: keep swap encrypted, or
  have none.

BUILD AND INSTALL

Plain C (C89), only libc, static. It uses seal's code from ../seal
(the archive, the crypto, common helpers), not a copy. install.sh
builds it into ~/.system/bin:

    ~/dotfiles/install.sh diary

By hand:

    make
    make test          (make -C ../seal first, for the unseal test)
    make PREFIX=$HOME/.system install

Files: main.c (options, settings), vault.c (opening, saving, wiping,
crashes), data.c (reading the diary's files, dates, plans), ui.c (the
screen, keys), term.c (the small terminal the editor runs in),
test/run.sh (the tests).
